title: '[pallet-revive] Derive CREATE1 from the transaction nonce only for the signer'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    A contract deployed without a salt by a substrate transaction now lands at
    `create1(address, nonce)` where `nonce` is the one the signer's `CheckNonce` consumed, and
    the signer's nonce is incremented once per deploy instead of twice. Every other origin
    (fallback accounts, proxies, multisigs, XCM sovereign accounts) derives from its own nonce
    and increments it, so its second deploy no longer fails with `DuplicateContract`.

    To tell the signer apart, `SetOrigin` now records the signer of a signed substrate
    transaction for the duration of its dispatch and charges the new
    `WeightInfo::set_origin_substrate_tx` weight for it. Required changes:

    - Keep `pallet_revive::evm::tx_extension::SetOrigin::<Runtime>::default()` in the
      transaction extension of every pipeline that dispatches signed substrate calls into
      pallet-revive. Without it the signer's deploy uses a different address than dry runs
      predict.
    - If the runtime uses `pallet-meta-tx`, append `SetOrigin<Runtime>` to the meta
      transaction extension tuple, after `CheckNonce`. Meta transactions then deploy at the
      meta signer's consumed nonce. The extension adds no bytes to the encoded meta
      transaction, but clients building one must include it in the signed extension list.
    - Implement `set_origin_substrate_tx` in the runtime's `pallet_revive::WeightInfo`, or
      regenerate the weights.
- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    A contract deployed without a salt by a substrate extrinsic lands at the address derived
    from the nonce the extrinsic was signed with, the same address `eth_getTransactionCount`
    and the dry run predict, and costs one nonce. Deploys from proxies, multisigs and fallback
    accounts each use and consume their own nonce.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: major
- name: kitchensink-runtime
  bump: major
